# ── Chapter 21 fidelity pins: "A Way to Be Heard" ──────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/21-a-way-to-be-heard.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The chapter's case lists three fixtures, which share no record or subject
# constant:
#   tests/pins/records/child_with_nobody/fixture.nibli
#   tests/pins/justice/hearing/positive
#   tests/pins/justice/appeal/request-without-operator-or-previous-permission
#
# The rest of the family is cited rather than duplicated:
#   tests/pins/justice/hearing/{CivilJustice,AdministrativeJustice,
#       FamilyJustice,LabourJustice,ConsumerJustice,ConstitutionalJustice,
#       CriminalJustice}   one case per domain the section enumerates
#   tests/pins/justice/{assistance,investigation,prosecution,defence,
#       survivor,restoration}/   the independent-assistance, function-separation
#                                and voluntary-restoration records
#   tests/pins/justice/{case-relief,general-invalidation,composition-review}/
#                                which court may order what
#   tests/pins/justice/enforcement/   enforcement bounded to the exact remedy
#   tests/pins/justice/nonresponse/positive   a certified failure to answer
#                                moves the duty to an independent alternate
#   tests/pins/justice/boundaries/no-person-consequences-or-forged-orders
#   tests/pins/justice/{access,conditions,continuity,release-review,defect}/
# None of them establishes that a hearing happened or that anybody was heard.
#
# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE: a FALSE is pinned only where the KB could have said
# TRUE, and the comment says what would flip it.
#
# THE CLOSING BLOCK IS LAST FOR A MECHANICAL REASON. It ends on an unscoped
# `:accept`, which leaves its rule resident for everything below it, so it sits
# at the foot of the file where there is nothing below to widen. Do not move it
# up, and do not scope it: the block's whole point is to ask what the accepted
# rule derived.

:expect-pins 25

# ── "A right needs a way to be heard when somebody denies it." ─────────────
? complete(JCaseRecord, JusticeFairHearing, JCaseCase).
# => TRUE

? obliged(JCaseOperator, ProvideFairReasonedAndContestableHearing, JCaseRecord).
# => TRUE

# ── "A request to review needs nobody's permission" ────────────────────────
# A bare request from anyone at all, with no operator authorisation and no
# previous reviewer, lands a review duty on an independent reader.
? obliged(IndependentJusticeReader, ReviewJusticeRequest, BareJusticeRequest).
# => TRUE

# ── "This non-coercive route creates no arrest warrant, search power,
# seizure power or authority to imprison." ────────────────────────────────
# The hearing stands and its subject is neither confined nor discredited.
? prisoner(JCaseSubject).
# => FALSE

? false(JCaseSubject).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, JCaseSubject).
# => FALSE

# "a representative does not create the child's standing" — and a hearing
# record does not create personhood either. Writing a birth or an encounter
# for this subject is what would flip it.
? person(JCaseSubject).
# => FALSE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "money, documentation and immigration status cannot be the price of entry"
# — the child holds none of the three and is owed the floor regardless.
? person(Nell).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Secure, Nell).
# => TRUE

# "money, documents and immigration status cannot be the price of entry" — the
# barrier saying so has `person($x)` as its whole body, so it holds for a child
# with one birth entry, no papers and nobody to ask on their behalf. Nothing
# about wealth, documents or representation appears in it to be missing.
? prevents(Nell, JusticeWealthDocumentationOrStatusConditionedAccess).
# => TRUE

? prevents(Nell, JusticeInstitutionalDelayExtendingCustody).
# => TRUE

# "What none of it supplies is the asking." Nothing has been filed for this
# child, so no hearing and no review duty follows for them.
? complete(Nell, JusticeFairHearing, Nell).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(Nell).
# => FALSE


# ── "Relief is the last of them ... the only one with an effect on a
# conviction." ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nia was cleared beside a generic Appeals judgment, so relief derives and the
# conviction does not hold. Ruk has no clearing entry, so it does.
? permits(Appeals, Nia).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Nia).
# => FALSE

? prisoner(Ruk).
# => TRUE

? permits(Appeals, Ruk).
# => FALSE

# Nothing has landed a duty on the appeal body for Ruk: that needs either a
# contested temporal defect or a justice appeal record, and the shipped cast
# carries neither.
? obliged(Appeals, Ruk).
# => FALSE

# ── The encoding that is refused, and what it actually said ────────────────
# Written as a right of appeal and read as one for a long time. It is not one:
# `permits(Appeals, ·)` is relief already granted, so this rule says every
# confined person has already won, and the conviction rule reads its absence.
# The loop is the conclusion feeding its own premise.
:refuse reasoning /Unstratifiable/
all $x: prisoner($x) -> permits(Appeals, $x).

# ── The encoding that is not refused ───────────────────────────────────────
# The same guarantee written as what it meant — a duty on the appeal body to
# hear every confined person's case — loads without complaint. Nothing reads
# it, so nothing downstream moves.
:accept
all $x: prisoner($x) -> obliged(Appeals, $x).

? obliged(Appeals, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# "an unheard appeal does not empty a conviction" — the duty is in force for
# every confined person and the conviction is untouched.
? prisoner(Ruk).
# => TRUE

? permits(Appeals, Ruk).
# => FALSE

? free(Ruk).
# => FALSE

# And the duty reaches only people the conviction rule reached. The child has
# no case to hear.
? obliged(Appeals, Nell).
# => FALSE
